Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), a kind of porous material comprised of metal ions and organic ligands primarily, have been investigated lately in a broad research field, such as gas storage and separation, catalysis, sensors, drug delivery, due to their fascinating merits of tunable metrics, porosity and organic functionality [12–16]. Recently, many researchers have fixed their attention on their application in the energy storage, especially in supercapacitors [17,18]. According to previous reports, MOFs could be applied as electrode materials of supercapacitors in various ways, for example, as templates via carbonization for EDLCs electrode [19,20], or as precursors to prepare metal oxides for pseudocapacitors [21,22]. MOFs can also be used for SCs directly, due to their unique structures able to provide plentiful pseudocapacitive redox centers [23]. However, the existence of some intrinsic defects, such as the pore sizes unfitting for the ion transportation, the poor electrical conductivity for the electric transfer [24], and the relative weak stability during charge–discharge process [25], will lead to a comparatively lower specific capacitance and poorer cycling stability of MOFs than those of the transition metal oxides. Fortunately, the inherent features, including tunable metrics, designable structure, and adjustable porosity as well as organic functionality for MOFs, endow them the specificity to change into a preferable structure for the utility in supercapacitors. Therefore, many approaches have been taken in to consideration to solve the problems [26,27]. For instance, Yang et al. [28] have prepared a layered structural Co-based MOF for supercapacitors with high specific capacitance, of which the layered structure provides enough space for \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) intercalation/de-intercalation process and acts as an electron transport channel to promote the electrochemical performance. Du et al. [29] have facilely fabricated a series of different structural Ni-MOFs by adjusting the molar ratio of metal ion and organic ligands and found that the optimal hierarchical porous structural Ni-MOF possesses a high specific capacitance of \(1057\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and keeps \(70\%\) of the original specific capacitance after 2500 cycles, due to its largest specific surface supplying more active sites for electrochemical reactions. Moreover, some dopants methods have been reported to improve MOFs' performance for supercapacitors, such as the graphene [30,31], polymers [32] and other metals [33].
In this work, we have synthesized a dandelion-like hollow Ni/Co-MOF through doping \({\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}\) to the original Ni-MOF by a facile hydrothermal synthesis. The doping cobalt ions would occupy the position of the nickel ions, which not only results in the decrease of crystallinity, but also alters the waxberry-like structure of Ni-MOF to a dandelion-like structure of Ni/Co-MOF. The less crystal structure and novel dandelion-like morphology are advantageous for the rate capacitance and cycling life improvement when it is used as the supercapacitor electrode material. The obtained Ni/Co-MOF possesses a high specific capacitance of \(758\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , with a superior rate performance of \(89\%\) retention at \(10\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , as well as a good cycling stability of \(75\%\) retention after 5000 cycles. To further study its practical application, the asymmetric supercapacitor Ni/Co-MOF//AC is assembled, which exhibits a high specific energy density of \(20.9\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at the power density of \(800\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) . This synthetic method provides a facile mixed-metal strategy to facilitate MOFs directly used in supercapacitors.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Preparation of Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF
The reagents used for the synthesis of the Ni-MOF, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) , 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid, polyvinylpyrrolidone K-30 (PVP) were purchased from Aladdin.
Some other frequently used reagents and solvents were used as supplied without further purification.
The Ni/Co-MOF was synthesized by a hydrothermal method. \(0.216\,\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(0.216\,\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) , \(0.15\,\mathrm{g}\) 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid, and \(1.5\;\mathrm{g}\) PVP were dissolved in \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) mixture solution with the volume ratio of water: ethanol: \(\mathsf{D M F=}\) 1:1:1 at room temperature under supersonic vibration for 30 min. The mixture solution was then transferred into a \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-line autoclave and kept at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\) . At last the purple product were washed with ethanol for three times and dried in vacuum at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight. This purple sample is denoted as Ni/Co-MOF. In contrast, the other sample was prepared by the similar method, in which the two metal salts were changed to \(0.432\,\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) as reported by Wang et al. [34]. This obtained green sample is denoted as Ni-MOF. The brief synthesis process is shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.2. Characterization of the Ni-MOFs
The structure of Ni-MOF was determined by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) using Cu-K \(\mathfrak{x}\) radiation. The microstructures of the samples were observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Hitachchi SU-8000), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) (HRTEM, Tecnai G2F20). The electronic state of ions was analysis by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) by Al-Kα as X-ray source (ESCALAB 250Xi). FT-IR was used to determine different functional groups in the materials by VIBTEX 80 V.
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurement
According to the following Eq. (1), the specific capacitance will be obtained,
\[
\mathsf{C}=\frac{\mathrm{I}\Delta\mathsf{t}}{\mathrm{m}\Delta\mathsf{V}}
\]
in which C \((\mathsf{F}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) stands for the specific capacitance; m (g) the mass of active material in one electrode; I (A) the charge/discharge current; \(\Delta\mathbf{t}\) (s) the discharge time; \(\Delta\mathsf{V}\) (V) the potential window.
The asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) Ni/Co-MOF/AC was assembled with Ni/Co-MOF as the positive electrode and AC as the negative electrode. The performance was tested in 2 M KOH electrolyte solution by a two-electrode electrochemical system. The mass ratio between the two electrode in ASC abides by the charge balance of \(\mathsf{q}^{+}=\mathsf{q}^{-}\). Thus the optimal mass ratio will follow the Eq. (2),
\[
\frac{m^{+}}{m^{-}}=\frac{c^{-}\times\Delta V^{-}}{c^{+}\times\Delta V^{+}}
\]
in which \(m^{+}\), \(\Delta V^{+}\), and \(c^{+}\)denote the mass, potential window and specific capacity of the positive electrode, respectively; and \(m^{-}\), \(\Delta V^{-}\), and \(c^{-}\)are the corresponding negative one, respectively.
The energy density (E, W \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\)) and power density (P, W \(\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\)) of the ASC are calculated by the equation as follows,
\[
E=\frac{1}{2}C\Delta V^2
\]
\[
P=\frac{\mathrm{E}}{\Delta t}
\]
in which C is the specific capacitance of the ACS calculated by the total weight of the positive and negative active materials; \(\Delta V\) (V) and \(\Delta t(s)\) are the voltage window and discharge time, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
The structures of the obtained MOFs are detected by the XRD and FT-IR. As shown in Fig. 2a, the primary XRD peaks of the obtained Ni-MOF are consistent well with that reported by Wang et al. [34]. To further confirm the results, we also refer to some other literatures. For example, Zou et al. [35] have prepared the Ni-MOF by the same method and we find that the XRD pattern agrees well with our results. Moreover, the obtained SEM images in Fig. 3(a, b) are similar with previous study, which further prove that we have successfully prepared the Ni-MOF as the literatures. It
is worth noting that the peaks of prepared Ni/Co-MOF almost disappear, indicating the low degree of crystallinity. The reason is that the \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) partial substituting for the \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \) in the Ni-MOF will destroy its initial crystal structure tremendously. In the FT-IR spectra as shown in Fig. 2b, the identical FT-IR band of the two samples confirms that the framework of the Ni/Co-MOF still remains the same functional groups and they are isostructure [36]. The absorbed band at 3592 and \( 3442~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) is ascribed to the \( \mathrm{O-H} \) stretching vibration, inferring the existence of the coordinated water linked in the MOFs [37]. Moreover, the strong bands at 1626 and 1578 \( \mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibration of coordinated \( \left(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\right) \) group and the bands at 1436 and \( 1374~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) belong to the symmetric stretching vibration [38].
Next, we use the SEM to further survey the MOFs' morphology. As shown in Fig. 3(a, b), the SEM image indicates that the Ni-MOF
exhibits a 3D waxberry-like structure in the average size as the literature reported [34]. It is worth noting that the surface of the Ni-MOF is rough and grows circular cone structures. In Fig. 3c, Ni/Co-MOF shows a dandelion-like hollow structure within the uneven sizes from 3 to \( 10\,\upmu\mathrm{m} \). Moreover, the magnification (Fig. 3d and inset) image shows the average nano-sized rods scatter on the shell surface disorderly and connect to each other.
To study the microstructure of the MOFs in depth, the TEM images are displayed in Fig. 4. As shown in Fig. 4a, Ni-MOF displays a hollow sphere structure with surface sparsely distributed with circular cones which are composed of aggregated nano-sized rods (Fig. 4b). As shown in Fig. 4c, the Ni/Co-MOF displays a sphere structure surrounded by scattered tiny rods, which completely conceal the hollow structure due to the rods special distribution. In the magnified TEM image (Fig. 4d), it can be
further observed that the arrangement mode of the tiny rods is more equally distributed in a scattered status than the aggregated status of Ni-MOF. In addition, the element mapping of C, O, Ni and Co in Fig. 4e indicates the homogeneous and average distribution of all the elements in the dandelion-like hollow structure.
The wide XPS scan results indicate the existence of C, O, N and Ni on the surface of Ni-MOF (Fig. 5a) and the additional Co on the surface of Ni/Co-MOF (Fig. 5b). To better identify the bonding states of Ni and Co, the Ni 2p and Co 2p spectra are also analyzed. In Fig. 5c, it can be found that the bonding state of the Ni 2p in two samples are identical, and the peaks centered at 855.7 and 873.5 eV can be assigned to the Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) corresponding to the spin-orbit characteristic of \(\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\) , and the two broad peaks at the 861.2 and \(879.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) are the satellite peaks of Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) , respectively [39]. For the Co 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 5d), the peaks at 780.1 and \(796.6\,\mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to Co \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) , corresponding to the spin-orbit characteristic of \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{2+}\) , and the two broad peaks at 785.1 and \(801.3\,\mathrm{eV}\) are the satellite peaks of Co \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) , respectively [40].
The electrochemical performance of the two samples is tested in 2 M KOH electrolyte in Fig. 6. As shown in Fig. 6a, the CV curves of Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF both have a pair of peak at a scan rate of \(10\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) within the window of \(0{-}0.6\,\mathrm{V}\) , due to the faradic behavior of the \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) intercalation-disintercalation in the MOFs. For the Ni/Co-MOF CV curve, only one pair of redox appearing is ascribed to the overlapping of the closed redox peaks. At the same scan rate, the specific capacitance is related to the scanning area of the CV curve. It can be observed that the area of Ni/Co-MOF is a little less than that of Ni-MOF, indicating that the former has a lower specific capacitance. Fig. 6b displays the CV curves of Ni/Co-MOF at various scan rates from 10 to \(50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . As the scan rates increasing, the anodic and cathodic peaks shift a little, indicating a low resistance of the electrode material and a good rate capability [41]. The GCD curves of the Ni/Co-MOF in Fig. 6c exhibit the almost asymmetrical charge-discharge platform within the potential window of 0–0.48
V, ascribed to the pseudocapacitance features. Based on the GCD curves, the specific capacitance are calculated from Eq. (1) to be 758, 757.4, 756.2, 731.2, 672.9, and \(643.7\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the current densities of 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, and \(15\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. In Fig. 6e, the specific capacitance of the Ni-MOF can reach \(781.2\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , which is better than that of Ni/Co-MOF, corresponding with the CV curves in Fig. 6a. However, the capacitance retention at \(10\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) of the Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF are \(60\%\) and \(89\%\) , respectively, indicating the latter with better rate capacitance, which matches well with the CV curves in Fig. 6b. This is because the scattered rods on the sphere of Ni/Co-MOF will provide more effective active reaction sites and reduce the ion diffusive impedance at the higher scanning rate. By contrast, the aggregated rods on Ni-MOF sphere surface will hinder the ion diffusion. The cycling stability is also an important parameter for supercapacitors. In Fig. 6e, it can be observed that the retention of Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF can reach \(45\%\) and \(75\%\) , respectively, after 5000 cycles at \(15\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , indicating the significant enhancement of the stability for Ni/Co-MOF. It can be inferred that the interconnected rods will support the structure to alleviate the structure collapse during the charge-discharge process. Moreover, it is reported that the disordered structure is beneficial for the enhancement of the electrochemical and structure stability [25], thus the low-crystalline material of Ni/Co-MOF caused by \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{2+}\) partially substituting for the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) will possess a higher rate capacitance and a better circling stability than the materials of Ni-MOF.
The EIS of Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF are shown in the Nyquist plots (Fig. 6f). The equivalent circuit is fitted as shown in Fig. 6f inset. In the high frequency range, the real axis intercept represents internal resistance (Rs), caused by the contact resistance of interface between active material and current collector, and the intrinsic resistance from the electrode and electrolyte. The Rs of the Ni-MOF and Ni/Co-MOF is 1.26 and \(0.8\,\Omega\) , respectively, indicating the latter with less resistance. And the semicircle at high frequency corresponds to the charge transfer resistance (Rt), caused by the faradic reactions ascribed to the conductivity of active
materials [42,43]. It is known that the larger the diameter is, the higher the impedance is. Here, the fitted Rt values are 5.49 and \(0.83\,\Omega\), respectively, in agreement with the Rt trend. Moreover, no obvious semicircle for the Ni/Co-MOF indicates its small charge transfer resistance in high frequency [44]. At low frequency, the slopes of the straight line are both larger than \(45^{\circ}\), indicating both materials exhibiting a good double-layer capacitor and fast ion transport [45]. According to the analysis, it can be seen that Ni/Co-MOF with lower Rs and Rb, which can be inferred that the scattered rods at the sphere surfaces will facilitate the electric transfer and ion diffusion thus reducing the impedance.
Based on the above mentioned, it can reach the conclusion that Ni/Co-MOF exhibits a better electrochemical performance in general. On account of the measurement and analysis, the reasons can be concluded as follows. First, \({\cal C}0^{2+}\) ions with smaller ionic radius \((0.65\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}})\) partially replace the position of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) \((0.69\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}})\) will create more defects in the original Ni-MOF structure, thus the formed amorphous structure will generate a great deal of free holes in the Ni/Co-MOF and contributes to the electrical conductivity [36]. Therefore, the intrinsic impedance will be reduced to a certain extent, corresponding with EIS (Fig. 6e). Second, according to the SEM and TEM images, the scattered rods distributed on the sphere surface will provide more effective active reaction sites at
higher current densities, accelerate the ion transportation and electrical transfer and act as a supporting role, therefore, enhancing the rate capacitance and cycling stability. However, the addition of the \({\cal C}0^{2+}\) also causes the wide range of size distribution, limiting the specific capacitance improvement on the contrary.
To further test the practical applications of Ni/Co-MOF, the ASC device is fabricated using Ni/Co-MOF as the positive electrode and active carbon (AC) as the negative electrode. The AC material, as a frequently-used electrode material in supercapacitors, exhibits a specific capacitance of \(99\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with little capacitance degradation after 5000 cycles in the three-electrode system [17]. The relative values of \(m^{+}\), \(\Delta V^{+}\), and \(c^{+}\)for the positive materials are \(1.26\,\mathrm{mg}\), \(0.48\,\mathrm{V}\), and \(758\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), while \(\Delta V\) and \(c\) for the negative materials are \(1\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(99\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Based on the specific capacitance values and potential windows, the optimal loading mass ratio between the Ni/Co-MOF and AC is calculated to be 0.27, according to Eq. (2). Therefore, the mass on the positive and negative electrode are \(1.26\,\mathrm{mg}\) and \(4.63\,\mathrm{mg}\), respectively. In Fig. 7a, the typical CV curves of the ASC device shows only a little distortion within the voltage window of \(0{-}1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) at various scanning rates from 10 to \(50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), indicating an excellent charge-storage reversibility of the ASC device. In the GCD curves (Fig. 7b), the specific capacitance could be calculated to be 58.8,
51.3, 46.8, 45 and \(44.3\;\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (based on active materials weight on both electrodes) at 1, 2, 3, 4, and \(5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, according to Eq. (1). The cycling performance of the ASC, measured at \(3\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) as shown in Fig. 7c, keeps \(85\%\) retention after 5000 cycling numbers, indicating its excellent cycling stability. The Ragone plots of ASC derived from the GCD curves based on the Eqs. (3) and (4), exhibit a high energy density of \(20.9\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at a power density of \(800\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), and still remain \(15.8\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(4000\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), which is higher than the previous reported supercapacitors, such as NiMOF//AC \((16.5\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(2078\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) [46], NiS HNPs//AC \((11.6\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(187.5\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) [47], Ni-Co oxide//AC \((10.8\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(474.4\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) [48], \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) /Ni foam//AC \((7.7\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(600\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) [49], and \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}/\mathrm{rGO}//\mathrm{AC}\) \((7.4\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(127\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) [50]. Further, the relatively flat curve indicates the high energy density retention with the increasing power output [51]. After the ASC fabricated to be solid electrodes, they could light up a LED and keep for about 1 min with 2 ASC devices in series, as shown in Fig. 7d inset.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have used a facile hydrothermal method to prepare Ni/Co-MOF. Our results clearly show that the doping cobalt ions successfully occupy the position of nickel ions, which not only results in the decrease of crystallinity, but also alters the waxberry-like structure of Ni-MOF to a dandelion-like structure of Ni/Co-MOF. The low-crystalline structure will facilitate the conductivity, and the scattered distributed rods on the sphere surface will accelerate the ion transportation and supporting the structure to improve the rate capacitance and cycling stability. The Ni/Co-MOF shows an excellent specific capacitance of \(758\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) in the three-electrode system. Comparing with NiMOF, the obtained Ni/Co-MOF has a better rate capacitance (89% retention at \(10\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) and cycling life (75% retention after 5000 circulations). Besides, the assembled asymmetric Ni/Co-MOF//AC exhibits a high specific energy density of \(20.9\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at the power density of \(800\,\mathrm{W}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). Nevertheless, compared with other
bimetal oxides the specific capacitance here still needs to be improved, due to the intrinsic defects of MOFs. Therefore, our future work will contribute to enhance the specific capacitance and cycling stability to further promote their practical applications, for instance, by elaborately selecting some other metal dopant elements (Zn, Fe or Mn) to facilitate the morphology evolution and capacitance improvement.
